# AIXSHOP v1.0 — Complete Remediation & Production Readiness

## Mission

Perform a complete, evidence-driven remediation of the existing AIXSHOP v1.0 codebase.

Repository: `https://github.com/Kunnapatara/AIXSHOP-v1.0`

The objective is to fix all confirmed outstanding defects, preserve all existing development progress, and bring the application to a verifiable production-launch standard.

This is an incremental remediation task, NOT a rewrite or replacement project.

**Core principle: ZERO LOSS OF DEVELOPMENT PROGRESS.**

Do not assume that a feature is complete merely because its endpoint, UI, migration, or test exists. Verify the complete implementation path, including database persistence, workspace isolation, error handling, and actual runtime behavior.

## 1. Mandatory Rules — Preserve Existing Work

1. Inspect the entire existing project structure before making changes.
2. Identify the current Git commit, existing implementation, migrations, tests, and project architecture.
3. Preserve all existing features, business logic, UI, API contracts, database records, migrations, and working integrations.
4. Do not delete, replace, simplify, or rewrite working modules merely to make implementation easier.
5. Do not introduce duplicate services, repositories, routes, database tables, or parallel implementations.
6. Reuse the existing architecture, interfaces, repositories, validation utilities, and test infrastructure.
7. Make small, targeted, backward-compatible changes.
8. Do not modify historical database migrations. Use new forward-only migrations when schema changes are necessary.
9. Do not remove or weaken existing tests to make the test suite pass.
10. Do not fabricate test results, database results, external API responses, or production verification.
11. Do not commit, push, publish, deploy, or modify the remote Git repository without explicit owner approval.
12. Do not expose credentials, access tokens, API keys, customer data, or sensitive environment variables in logs or reports.

Before modifying any file, establish a baseline and identify the exact files and dependencies involved.

If an existing implementation conflicts with a proposed fix, preserve the existing behavior unless a specific defect requires changing it. Document the compatibility implications.

